2026-07-22T10:29:31.9915200Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9915569Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9915885Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9916059Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9916264Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9916437Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9916647Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9916823Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9917040Z         def inc(x):
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9917236Z             return x + 1
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9917422Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9917575Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9917738Z         def test_answer():
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9917947Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9918141Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9918294Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9918448Z     To execute it::
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9918629Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9918851Z         $ pytest
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919084Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919179Z         collected 1 items
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919253Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919338Z         test_sample.py F
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919411Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919535Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919668Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919742Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919832Z             def test_answer():
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919913Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9919999Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9920085Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9920154Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9920257Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9920388Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9920456Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9920534Z     
2026-07-22T10:29:31.9921093Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
